# LEVEL 13 → 14 — SSH AUTHENTICATION WITH A PRIVATE KEY


## LEVEL GOAL
Use a private SSH key file to log into `bandit14` and read the password from `/etc/bandit_pass/bandit14`.

## DESCRIPTION
Instead of providing a password, this level gives you a private SSH key file. SSH key-based authentication is the standard in professional environments — used by system administrators, cloud engineers, and DevOps teams to access Linux servers securely.

## APPROACH

**Step 1 — Inspect the home directory**

```bash
ls -l
```

The file `sshkey.private` was present.

**Step 2 — Copy the key to /tmp (home directory is read-only)**

```bash
cd /tmp && mkdir dir1
cp ~/sshkey.private /tmp/dir1/
cd /tmp/dir1
```

**Step 3 — Set correct file permissions**

```bash
chmod 600 sshkey.private
```

SSH will refuse to use a private key with insecure permissions. `chmod 600` restricts access to the owner only.

## Permission Breakdown

| Value | Meaning |

| `6` (owner)  | read + write  |
| `0` (group)  | no permission |
| `0` (others) | no permission |

**Step 4 — Log in using the key**

```bash
ssh -i sshkey.private bandit14@localhost -p 2220
```

**Step 5 — Read the password**

```bash
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit14
```

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## KEY TAKEAWAY
SSH key pairs work on asymmetric cryptography — the server holds the public key, you hold the private key. If they match, access is granted without a password. This concept applies directly to AWS EC2 access, GitHub SSH authentication, and production server management.
